# Commit after every prompt

When you finish the work requested in a user message, **create a git commit** if the repository has any changes to commit (modified, added, or deleted tracked files, or relevant untracked files that belong in the repo).

Do this at the end of the turn, after the task is done—not before verifying the change works when verification was part of the request.

## When not to commit

- No file changes resulted from the prompt (question-only, review-only, or purely informational replies).
- The only changes are build artifacts that should stay ignored (e.g. `dist/`, `node_modules/`).
- Files likely contain secrets (`.env`, credentials); never commit those—warn the user instead.
- The user explicitly says not to commit in that message.

## How to commit

Follow the repository **Git Safety Protocol** from user rules:

- Never update git config; no destructive git commands unless explicitly requested.
- Run `git status`, `git diff`, and `git log -1` (or recent log) to draft an accurate message.
- Stage only files related to the work; do not commit ignored generated output unless the user asked to track it.
- Use a concise 1–2 sentence message focused on **why**.
- On Windows PowerShell, pass the message safely (e.g. `git commit -m "message"` or a here-string)—do not use interactive git flags.
- After commit, run `git status` to confirm success.
- If a pre-commit hook fails, fix the issue and create a **new** commit; do not amend unless user rules allow it.
- Do **not** push unless the user asks.

## Scope

One prompt → at most one commit summarizing that prompt’s changes. Do not batch unrelated prior uncommitted work into the same commit unless it was all part of the same user request.
